.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt PDF2JPG 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm pdf2jpg
.Nd turn PDF pages into JPEGs at a pixel size, under an optional byte cap
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl cgL
.Op Fl e Ar engine
.Op Fl f Ar first
.Op Fl i Ar icc
.Op Fl l Ar last
.Op Fl n Ar mode
.Op Fl o Ar dir
.Op Fl p Ar name
.Op Fl q Ar quality
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl w Ar px
.Op Fl x Ar n
.Ar file.pdf
.Nm
.Fl h
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
renders the pages of a PDF file and writes each one as a JPEG whose
longest edge is a given number of pixels, optionally under a given
number of bytes.
Each stage is done by the tool best at it:
.Bl -tag -width "downsample"
.It rasterise
.Xr pdftoppm 1 ,
for sharp text and anti-aliased vector graphics, or
.Xr gs 1
with
.Fl e Ar gs ,
for correct CMYK and ICC handling.
.It downsample
.Xr magick 1 ,
with a Lanczos filter, optionally in linear light
.Pq Fl L .
.It encode
mozjpeg's
.Xr cjpeg 1 ,
else libjpeg's
.Xr cjpeg 1 ,
else
.Xr magick 1 .
.El
.Pp
Only the tools a run needs are required, but
.Xr pdftoppm 1
always is: one cheap pass at 72 dpi, where a pixel is a point, gives
the size of every page, mixed page sizes included.
A page is then rendered at
.Fl x
times the target and downsampled to it, and is rendered once: the
search for a quality under the
.Fl s
cap encodes it again, it does not render it again.
The search is a binary one, from quality 15 up to the
.Fl q
ceiling, and keeps the highest quality whose file fits.
.Pp
Each page is written as
.Ar dir Ns / Ns Ar name Ns - Ns Ar NN Ns .jpg ,
its number padded with zeros to the width of the page count, two
digits at least, and a one-page document as
.Ar dir Ns / Ns Ar name Ns .jpg .
Each file written is reported on stdout as
.Ql path  WxH px  q=Q  N bytes .
When no
.Xr cjpeg 1
is found, or the one found is not mozjpeg's, a note on stderr says
the files will be larger.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-q quality"
.It Fl c
Subsample the chroma 4:2:0, for smaller and softer files, instead of
keeping it whole.
.It Fl e Ar engine
Rasterise with
.Ql poppler ,
the default, or with
.Ql gs .
.It Fl f Ar first
The first page to write, a positive integer; the default is 1.
.It Fl g
Write grayscale JPEGs.
.It Fl h
Print the usage and exit.
.It Fl i Ar icc
The output ICC profile, handed to Ghostscript; it needs
.Fl e Ar gs .
.It Fl L
Downsample in linear light, which suits photographs.
.It Fl l Ar last
The last page to write, a positive integer; the default is the last
page.
A range that selects no page is an error.
.It Fl n Ar mode
Whether a file name carries the page number:
.Ql auto ,
the default, numbers the pages of a document of more than one page;
.Ql always
numbers them all, and
.Ql never
none, which is refused when more than one page is selected.
.It Fl o Ar dir
The directory to write to, created if need be; the default is the
current one.
.It Fl p Ar name
The file name before
.Ql -NN.jpg ;
the default is the PDF's own name, without its extension.
.It Fl q Ar quality
The JPEG quality, from 1 to 100, or with
.Fl s
the ceiling of the search; the default is 92.
.It Fl s Ar size
The largest file a page may make, in bytes, or with a unit:
.Ql 500k ,
.Ql 500kb
and
.Ql 1.5m
are 1024 and 1048576 times the number.
.It Fl w Ar px
The longest edge, in pixels; the default is 2000.
.It Fl x Ar n
Render at
.Ar n
times the target, then downsample; a number of 1 or more, the default
2.
At 1, without
.Fl L ,
the page is encoded as rendered.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It 0
Every page selected was written.
.It 1
A failure: a tool missing, a page that cannot be rendered, named by its
number, or an unreadable input.
.It 2
A usage error.
.It 3
Every page was written, but at least one stays over the
.Fl s
cap even at the lowest quality; each such file is named on stderr.
.It 130
Interrupted.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Every page at 2000 pixels on its longest edge, in the current
directory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pdf2jpg report.pdf
.Ed
.Pp
The first three pages, 1200 pixels and at most 300 KB each, into
.Pa out :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pdf2jpg -w 1200 -s 300k -l 3 -o out report.pdf
.Ed
.Pp
A CMYK brochure, through Ghostscript with a profile of one's own:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ pdf2jpg -e gs -i sRGB.icc brochure.pdf
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr cjpeg 1 ,
.Xr gs 1 ,
.Xr magick 1 ,
.Xr pdftoppm 1
